1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,199
Truckers do not live inside your VPN.
Their mobile app hits the harbor API from
a highway shoulder, proves identity
without a sticky HTTP session store shared
across pods, and moves on.

2
00:00:12,319 --> 00:00:28,280
Session cookies that assume one browser
and one server get awkward fast. A common
answer is a bearer token — and the usual
format is JWT. A JSON Web Token is three
Base64url segments: header, payload,
signature.

3
00:00:28,399 --> 00:00:35,600
The payload carries claims — subject,
expiry, issuer, scopes, custom fields like
trucker id.

4
00:00:35,719 --> 00:00:43,000
The signature binds those claims to a key
so a resource server can validate the
token without calling the issuer on every
request.

5
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:58,199
That is the appeal and the danger:
validation is local and fast, but a leaked
token is a stolen identity until it
expires. Spring Security’s OAuth2 Resource
Server support treats JWTs as first-class
credentials.

6
00:00:58,319 --> 00:01:14,199
On each request, a filter looks for
Authorization: Bearer ..., parses the JWT,
validates signature and claims, and builds
an Authentication — often a
JwtAuthenticationToken — into the
SecurityContext.

7
00:01:14,319 --> 00:01:29,519
Authorization rules then run against
authorities mapped from scopes or roles in
the token. Look at this java for a moment.
Narrate failure modes as carefully as the
happy path. Missing header → 401.
Malformed JWT → 401.

8
00:01:29,639 --> 00:01:45,679
Bad signature → 401. Expired exp → 401.
Valid token but wrong scope for
/api/admin/** → 403. Those statuses still
mean what they meant in authorization;
only the credential shape changed.

9
00:01:45,799 --> 00:01:57,879
Do not treat JWTs as encrypted blobs. The
payload is readable to anyone who holds
the token. Put secrets in the token only
if you accept that readers can see them —
which means do not.

10
00:01:57,999 --> 00:02:08,839
Prefer opaque references or short-lived
access tokens with minimal claims. Rotate
signing keys through a JWKS endpoint so
resource servers refresh keys without
redeploy theater.

11
00:02:08,959 --> 00:02:22,719
A topic-specific misconception is “we use
JWT, so we are doing OAuth2.” JWT is a
token format. OAuth2 is a delegation
protocol that often issues JWTs as access
tokens.

12
00:02:22,839 --> 00:02:29,839
You can validate JWTs without the full
OAuth2 dance, and you can run OAuth2 with
opaque tokens.

13
00:02:29,959 --> 00:02:40,679
Another misconception is stuffing every
trucker attribute into the access token
until it rivals a session record — then
wondering why logout and permission
changes feel impossible.

14
00:02:40,799 --> 00:02:47,959
Short TTL plus refresh (when you have a
real authorization server) beats a forever
token with a giant claim set.

15
00:02:48,079 --> 00:03:01,919
The bearer path is now concrete:
Authorization header → parse → signature
and claim checks → SecurityContext →
authority matchers. Format alone does not
finish the story. Who issues these tokens?

16
00:03:02,099 --> 00:03:09,579
How does a trucker consent? How does a
client obtain them without embedding
passwords? That is the OAuth2
conversation.
